Z||000000||dennis_6||10-20-2001||02:08 AM||||My brothers Fiero died again.  This time it cranks, but has no spark.  Changed ign module, coil, rotor button, and dis. cap.  I also tested the distributor coil and it was fine.  The car doesn't even have spark at coil.  Is there anything else that could be wrong?  Am I overlooking something.  The car is a 1984 2.5L automatic and it died on the expressway on his way home from college.  He said his engine just stopped.  Any information would help a lot.  Thanks!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-20-2001||02:17 AM||||Uh oh....take the distributor cap off and crank the engine and watch the rotor.<P>No spin = bad news...timing gear done.<P>Post results to move on to the next thing.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||dennis_6||10-20-2001||03:12 AM||||That will have to wait till morning.  But I don't think it moved.  I took the cap off wiggled the wires to the ign module to check for loose connections while my brother cranked the car.  I do not remeber the rotor cap moving.  If the timing gear is shot.  How serious is this?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||10-20-2001||05:27 AM||||Pretty bad. It can be replaced with the eng in the car, but it is a pain. You need to remove the cam. not for the faint of heart. It is hard to access. Remove the right rear wheel, to help you see it, along with the splash shield. Loosen the alternator, and anything else that has a belt, in order to get the v-belts off Pull the crankshaft pulley and hub by removing the big bolt holding it on. Remove the timing cover bolts.(some are longer than others, pay attention to where they go). The cover may be stuck on pretty good, stuck with rtv probably. You will probably have to pry it off withou bending it all up. Gear is behind the cover. You're supposed to remove the cam to get the gear off and back on, but I've heard of cutting the old gear off with a grinder of some kind, heating the new gear while cooling down the cam. This will allow you to get the expanded gear on over the shrunken cam shaft. Do a archive search on Bad timing gear. There are two threads on it I believe. I think it was Chris McDonald who had posted about having good luck doing this. good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||dennis_6||10-20-2001||07:57 AM||||It is the timing gear.  I think the best way to go is a cheap 2.5.  If you know if 2.5s in vehicles other than the Fiero can be used in an 84 auto please let me know!!!<P>I have a new post on this subject.<P>Thanks for all who responded!||||reg||1
